1002	Scriptura : Journal for Contextual Hermeneutics in Southern Africa	University of Stellenbosch	\N	https://scriptura.journals.ac.za/, https://translate.google.com/translate?hl=sw&sl=en&u=https://scriptura.journals.ac.za/&prev=search&pto=aue, https://journals.co.za/journal/script	f	\N	t	7	f	0	f	nan	f	\N	t	f	f	f	t	f	'Scriptura: Journal for Contextual Hermeneutics in Southern Africa' is a scholarly publication dedicated to exploring the multifaceted intersections of biblical interpretation and the unique cultural, social, and political realities of Southern Africa. Its scope encompasses a wide range of topics, including biblical studies, theology, ethics, and social justice, all framed within the lens of contextual hermeneutics. The journal seeks to foster a dialogue between academic scholarship and the lived experiences of communities in the region, aiming to contribute to a more relevant and impactful understanding of the Bible within Southern African contexts.\n\nThe journal's key themes revolve around the application of hermeneutical principles to the interpretation of scripture within the specificities of Southern African cultures, societies, and histories. This includes grappling with issues of colonialism, postcoloniality, race, gender, poverty, and conflict, among others. 'Scriptura' encourages scholars to engage with the biblical text in ways that are sensitive to the challenges and opportunities of the region, striving to produce scholarship that is both intellectually rigorous and socially relevant.\n\nThe target audience for 'Scriptura' includes academics, theologians, pastors, students, and anyone interested in the intersection of biblical interpretation and Southern African contexts. The journal aims to bridge the gap between theoretical scholarship and practical application, providing insights and resources for both academic and pastoral communities. By fostering dialogue and collaboration between scholars and practitioners, 'Scriptura' seeks to contribute to a more nuanced and contextualized understanding of the Bible in Southern Africa.\n\n'Scriptura' has made significant contributions to the field of contextual hermeneutics in Southern Africa. The journal has published numerous articles and book reviews that have advanced critical scholarship on issues such as liberation theology, gender and the Bible, and the role of the church in social transformation. Its impact extends beyond academia, as its publications have informed the work of various organizations and individuals engaged in social justice and development work in the region. The journal's commitment to contextualized biblical interpretation has empowered communities to engage with the Bible in ways that are relevant to their lives and aspirations, contributing to a more inclusive and transformative understanding of scripture. \n	10	5	2	5	\N
